public class AccountCountrySync {
    public static void handleAccountUpdate(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldAccountsMap) {
        // Identify accounts where BillingCountry changed
        Set<Id> accountsWithCountryChange = new Set<Id>();
        for (Account newAcc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountsMap.get(newAcc.Id);
            if (oldAcc != null && newAcc.BillingCountry != oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                accountsWithCountryChange.add(newAcc.Id);
            }
        }

        if (accountsWithCountryChange.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Query contacts only for accounts where BillingCountry changed
        // This is bulk-safe (one query, no queries in loops) and avoids hitting the 50k row limit
        Map<Id, List<Contact>> contactsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();
        for (Contact c : [SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :accountsWithCountryChange]) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                if (!contactsByAccount.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                    contactsByAccount.put(c.AccountId, new List<Contact>());
                }
                contactsByAccount.get(c.AccountId).add(c);
            }
        }

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Account newAcc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountsMap.get(newAcc.Id);
            if (oldAcc == null) {
                continue;
            }

            // Check if BillingCountry changed
            if (newAcc.BillingCountry != oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                List<Contact> contacts = contactsByAccount.get(newAcc.Id);
                if (contacts != null) {
                    for (Contact c : contacts) {
                        // Update if MailingCountry is blank or equals old BillingCountry
                        if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry) || c.MailingCountry == oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                            c.MailingCountry = newAcc.BillingCountry;
                            contactsToUpdate.add(c);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update contactsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**Explanation of Changes:**

The original approach of querying ALL 2.3 million contacts in a single SOQL query violates Salesforce's 50,000 record governor limit. The test failure showed that the implementation was querying too many rows.

I modified the approach to:
1. First identify which accounts have a `BillingCountry` change
2. Query contacts **only** for those affected accounts (using `WHERE AccountId IN :accountsWithCountryChange`)
3. This maintains bulk-safety (no queries in loops, only one query) while respecting governor limits

This approach still follows the spirit of the requirement ("one query, done" and "no queries in loops") but is practical for large orgs.

